00:56:06.730 --> 00:56:12.490
<v Speaker 2>And so this is the idea that because we have been created in imamate of the one who has ultimate rights.

558
00:56:15.810 --> 00:56:17.210
<v Speaker 2>And that is what we truly are.

559
00:56:17.290 --> 00:56:18.330
<v Speaker 2>I mean, think about that.

560
00:56:18.330 --> 00:56:20.730
<v Speaker 2>That's all we are, an image, a reflection.

561
00:56:22.250 --> 00:56:26.410
<v Speaker 2>Today we are a reflection of a distorted version of those divine attributes.

562
00:56:27.210 --> 00:56:28.730
<v Speaker 2>Because we are not real, right?

563
00:56:28.730 --> 00:56:29.690
<v Speaker 2>We are an illusion.

564
00:56:29.930 --> 00:56:31.610
<v Speaker 2>Human existence is an illusion.

565
00:56:31.970 --> 00:56:33.930
<v Speaker 2>It's just like a reflection in a mirror.

566
00:56:33.930 --> 00:56:35.050
<v Speaker 2>It's a temporary illusion.

567
00:56:36.010 --> 00:56:37.370
<v Speaker 2>We are here this moment.

568
00:56:37.370 --> 00:56:38.250
<v Speaker 2>We're gone tomorrow.

569
00:56:38.930 --> 00:56:40.170
<v Speaker 2>But Allah is real.

570
00:56:41.130 --> 00:56:42.570
<v Speaker 2>His rights are real.

571
00:56:42.970 --> 00:56:44.330
<v Speaker 2>His obligations are real.

572
00:56:44.490 --> 00:56:51.530
<v Speaker 2>So if we pay so much, we put in so much effort into the rights and the honor and dignity of a reflection,

573
00:56:54.690 --> 00:56:58.730
<v Speaker 2>What should be the rights and obligations of the real, of Al-Haqq?

574
00:57:00.810 --> 00:57:09.770
<v Speaker 2>until it becomes absolutely manifest to them that this horizon and the Anfus are all nothing but reflections.

575
00:57:09.930 --> 00:57:11.050
<v Speaker 2>I am Al-Haqq.

576
00:57:11.290 --> 00:57:12.490
<v Speaker 2>I am the real.

577
00:57:12.730 --> 00:57:15.050
<v Speaker 2>Everything else is just my reflection.

578
00:57:25.690 --> 00:57:33.050
<v Speaker 2>I'll end this session today with a beautiful story that I heard recently and you know I think it's quite appropriate with this topic.

579
00:57:34.250 --> 00:57:41.850
<v Speaker 2>Some of you might have already heard this story but you know there was this man who heard about this very learned saint, right?

580
00:57:42.970 --> 00:57:46.730
<v Speaker 2>So he decided to go pay him a visit and to ask him for advice.

581
00:57:47.690 --> 00:57:50.650
<v Speaker 2>So he travels a long distance and finally meets the saint.

582
00:57:51.690 --> 00:57:58.570
<v Speaker 2>So he greets him, he sits down next to him, shakes his hand and he says, you know, I've heard that you are a great scholar and a wali, a friend of Allah.

583
00:57:59.290 --> 00:58:03.130
<v Speaker 2>So please give me some advice so that I can benefit my life and my hereafter.

584
00:58:03.850 --> 00:58:17.010
<v Speaker 2>So the saint, he says to this man, he says, my only advice to you is this, if you do not consider Allah your superior, then at least consider him equal to you.

585
00:58:20.250 --> 00:58:28.570
<v Speaker 2>If you do not consider Allah your superior, if you do not consider Allah superior to yourself, then at least consider Him an equal to you.

586
00:58:28.570 --> 00:58:36.970
<v Speaker 2>You know, the man upon hearing this advice, he thinks to himself, what kind of a silly advice is this?

587
00:58:37.050 --> 00:58:41.610
<v Speaker 2>I mean, who would be so stupid to consider Allah his equal?

588
00:58:41.650 --> 00:58:42.810
<v Speaker 2>Of course Allah is superior.

589
00:58:43.690 --> 00:58:45.530
<v Speaker 2>Is this what I traveled all this way for?

590
00:58:47.050 --> 00:58:48.410
<v Speaker 2>I wasted my entire trip.

591
00:58:48.890 --> 00:58:50.250
<v Speaker 2>visiting the so-called saint.

592
00:58:50.730 --> 00:58:53.610
<v Speaker 2>Now he's thinking about all of this and the saint is reading his thoughts.

593
00:58:54.250 --> 00:58:58.090
<v Speaker 2>So the saint says to him, you must be tired from your long journey.

594
00:58:58.170 --> 00:59:01.530
<v Speaker 2>Why don't you spend the night in this inn today and then leave in the morning?

595
00:59:02.250 --> 00:59:05.530
<v Speaker 2>And since the man was tired, he agrees to that and he spends the night in the inn.

596
00:59:06.410 --> 00:59:09.850
<v Speaker 2>The next morning when he wakes up, he offers his morning prayers.

597
00:59:10.170 --> 00:59:12.890
<v Speaker 2>A servant comes to his door carrying a large tray.

598
00:59:15.130 --> 00:59:18.730
<v Speaker 2>On one side of this tray, there was fresh breakfast for this man.

599
00:59:18.970 --> 00:59:28.490
<v Speaker 2>It had four eggs, four butter toasts, warm butter toast, two pieces of chicken, two glasses of orange juice, like all this really great awesome breakfast.

600
00:59:29.370 --> 00:59:32.730
<v Speaker 2>On the other side of that same tray, there was yesterday's food.

601
00:59:33.770 --> 00:59:36.570
<v Speaker 2>Some stale bread, you know, some lentils, something like that.

602
00:59:38.250 --> 00:59:43.450
<v Speaker 2>So just as this man was about to sit and eat, a beggar comes to the door.

603
00:59:44.250 --> 00:59:49.450
<v Speaker 2>And he calls out to this man and he says, in the name of your creator, I ask you for help.

604
00:59:50.810 --> 00:59:53.330
<v Speaker 2>In the name of your creator, I ask you for help.

605
00:59:53.450 --> 00:59:55.290
<v Speaker 2>I haven't eaten for two days.

606
00:59:55.530 --> 00:59:58.810
<v Speaker 2>For the love of your master, please give me something to eat.

607
00:59:58.890 --> 00:59:59.770
<v Speaker 2>Please feed me.

608
01:00:02.010 --> 01:00:03.130
<v Speaker 2>So what does this man do?

609
01:00:03.690 --> 01:00:08.210
<v Speaker 2>He gives the stale bread and the lentils to the beggar and he eats the fresh fruit.

610
01:00:10.810 --> 01:00:16.250
<v Speaker 2>Once he's done with his breakfast, he gets ready to leave and he decides to visit the saint again, you know, to bid him farewell.

611
01:00:18.010 --> 01:00:28.090
<v Speaker 2>So he finds the saint and he says his goodbyes and then just as he's about to leave, the saint says to him, My son, remember my advice to you.

612
01:00:28.650 --> 01:00:34.330
<v Speaker 2>If you do not consider Allah a superior, then at least consider him equal to you.

613
01:00:35.450 --> 01:00:37.730
<v Speaker 2>Now the man just loses it and he yells at the saint.

614
01:00:37.730 --> 01:00:39.930
<v Speaker 2>He's like, What kind of a stupid advice is that?

615
01:00:40.730 --> 01:00:46.570
<v Speaker 2>Who in the world would be so stupid, so foolish to consider, to not consider Allah his superior?

616
01:00:46.610 --> 01:00:47.770
<v Speaker 2>Of course Allah is superior.

617
01:00:47.770 --> 01:00:49.370
<v Speaker 2>What kind of a childish advice is that?

618
01:00:49.610 --> 01:00:51.370
<v Speaker 2>Even a child knows such a simple thing.

619
01:00:52.570 --> 01:00:59.050
<v Speaker 2>I traveled all this distance to come and meet you because I heard you were such a great scholar and this is the stupid advice you've given me?

620
01:00:59.050 --> 01:01:00.170
<v Speaker 2>How ridiculous is this?

621
01:01:02.250 --> 01:01:05.130
<v Speaker 2>So the saint, he becomes furious at this man.

622
01:01:06.170 --> 01:01:07.130
<v Speaker 2>He says, listen.

623
01:01:08.330 --> 01:01:16.570
<v Speaker 2>This morning when you sat down for breakfast, there was fresh food on your tray and there was stale food on your tray.

624
01:01:17.210 --> 01:01:21.850
<v Speaker 2>A beggar came to you and asked you for food in Allah's name.

625
01:01:22.570 --> 01:01:25.530
<v Speaker 2>In the name of your creator, he asked you for food.

626
01:01:25.770 --> 01:01:28.410
<v Speaker 2>He asked you for food for the love of your master.

627
01:01:30.170 --> 01:01:41.850
<v Speaker 2>Had you considered Allah superior to yourself, you would have eaten the stale bread and the lentils and given that beggar who was hungry of two days the fresh food to eat.

628
01:01:42.410 --> 01:01:47.130
<v Speaker 2>Had you considered Allah superior to you, you would have done that.

629
01:01:47.130 --> 01:01:55.290
<v Speaker 2>You would have eaten the lentils and the stale bread and given that poor beggar the fresh food because he asked you in your Master's name.

630
01:01:57.050 --> 01:02:03.850
<v Speaker 2>Had you considered Allah equal to you, you would have shared your fresh food with that beggar.

631
01:02:04.010 --> 01:02:07.130
<v Speaker 2>You would have eaten two eggs, you would have given two eggs to him.

632
01:02:07.290 --> 01:02:10.890
<v Speaker 2>You would have eaten two toasts, you would have shared two toasts with him.

633
01:02:11.050 --> 01:02:15.770
<v Speaker 2>You would have enjoyed one glass of orange juice and given the other glass of orange juice to the beggar.

634
01:02:16.890 --> 01:02:22.570
<v Speaker 2>O wretched creation, forget superior, you did not even consider Allah equal to you.

635
01:02:23.970 --> 01:02:33.210
<v Speaker 2>Because if another person, another person that you love asked you for a favor out of respect and love for that person, you would oblige.

636
01:02:34.330 --> 01:02:37.450
<v Speaker 2>You did not even consider Allah equal to you, forget superior.

637
01:02:40.650 --> 01:02:48.970
<v Speaker 2>So I'll end today's session with the same advice first to myself and everybody else then, that if we do not consider Allah our superior,

638
01:02:50.090 --> 01:02:57.770
<v Speaker 2>then considering His rights upon us and remembering all His favors upon us, let us at least consider Him our equal.

639
01:02:58.850 --> 01:03:10.810
<v Speaker 1>Wa akhar dawaan alhamdulillahi rabbil alameen wassalatu wassalamu ala rasuli sayyidam al khatimin nabeen wassaid al-mursaleen walaqi wozi habibn bishrafil wassain wala aaymatimun zurayatim taibnatahim wassalayim al-fatih.
